A hidden, experimental `--member-invariants` option, requiring `--type-system-refresh`, begins member invariants (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/105). A datatype, class, trait, newtype or abstract type may declare `invariant e` members; they are parsed, printed and resolved as ghost predicates of the type, with the plan's reads restriction (an invariant depends on no mutable state); an invariant is never opaque. A codatatype may not declare an invariant, and a program, libraries included, that declares a member invariant may not declare a codatatype. For a datatype with invariants, `raw D` names the type of all its values, and `D` the subset of them that satisfy the invariants: constructor applications and updates have type `raw D`, and the members that an invariant applies to a raw value are inferred to be raw members, in which `this` has type `raw D`. The invariants of an inductive datatype, declared or inherited, outside a refining module, are verified: converting a `raw D` to `D`, and applying a member that is not raw to a `raw D`, are checked against each invariant. For a generic datatype, `D<X>` has the variance that `D` declares, as `raw D<X>` does, and the resolver checks that the invariants, with the functions and constants they call, are monotone in each covariant type parameter and antitone in each contravariant one, by the polarity of each use: a universal quantifier over the parameter is negative, an existential quantifier or a type test positive; an arrow's parameter type, a negation or an antecedent flips the polarity; an equivalence, a condition, a comprehension, a non-variant type argument or a function whose body is not known uses it both ways; a generic function contributes its parameters' polarity in its body; a statement in an expression does not count. A conversion against the variance is checked. A datatype that declares or inherits invariants may declare one `witness e` (or `ghost witness e`) member, which makes `D` auto-initializable (or nonempty), and gives compiled code its default value; without one, `D` is possibly empty. The resolver checks that an invariant does not refer to the set of values of its own type (the plan's extension condition), conservatively, with the dependence of each type argument of a generic type that the invariant reaches. The invariants of a class, declared or inherited, outside a refining module, are verified too: a constructor checks them at `new;`, before which the object is a `raw C`, and every non-null value of `C` or `C?` satisfies them; a class with invariants must declare a constructor and may not declare a witness; `raw C` is a subtype of `raw C?` and of `raw P` for each parent trait `P` with invariants, and of nothing else, so a raw reference reaches `object`, a trait or a collection of those only where it is proved to satisfy all the invariants of its class; `o is C` tests the dynamic type alone, and a test of a raw reference is ghost. The invariants of a trait, reference or general, are verified too: every type that extends it inherits them, each ancestor once, in a post-order walk of the extends clauses, and checks them where it constructs its values; every value of the trait type satisfies them; `raw P` is the type of all the values of the types that extend `P`, and a `raw P` is checked against the invariants of `P` alone, so a test of a general trait's value against a datatype that extends it is ghost, while one of a reference against a class or trait compiles; two types join at a trait with invariants if neither is raw, and at its raw type otherwise. Each invariant is well formed where those before it hold: the inherited ones, then the type's own earlier ones. An invariant is revealed wherever its type is, and nowhere else. A program that declares an invariant of a newtype or abstract type, a newtype or abstract type that extends a trait with invariants, or a type with invariants in a refining module, is not verified: the verifier reports those declarations as not yet supported, and compiled code ignores invariants. `raw` for a newtype or abstract type, and witness members of traits, newtypes and abstract types, are parsed but not yet supported. A `.doo` library built with the option requires it of its clients; one built without it is unchanged. Without the option, parsing, diagnostics and the generated Boogie are unchanged.
